static const UNRELEASED: Omit<ChangelogEntry, "version" | "date"> = {
  title: "CLI guards: no more installing into Marko 5, Tailwind v3, or React projects",
  summary:
    "Three wrong-project shapes the CLI used to accept and silently mis-configure are now hard refusals with the fix spelled out: a Marko that cannot reach 6, a Tailwind v3 setup, and a components.json that belongs to shadcn/ui for React.",
  changes: [
    "Breaking: `init` refuses a project whose `marko` cannot satisfy Marko 6 (`^5`, `~5`, `5.37.0`, `<6`; the installed version in node_modules wins when readable), exiting 1 with 'marko-ui requires Marko 6' and the official upgrade/interop guide (https://markojs.com/docs/guide/marko-5-interop). Ranges that allow 6 (`>=5`, `*`, `latest`, `workspace:`) pass, and `--force` bypasses — the same escape hatch as the non-Marko refusal. Previously init installed Marko 6 tags-API components into Marko 5 (class API) projects.",
    "Breaking: `init` refuses a Tailwind v3 project — the nearest installed or declared `tailwindcss` (resolved walking up to the workspace root, so a hoisted monorepo app is judged by the workspace's Tailwind, not by its own empty package.json) cannot reach 4, or a `tailwind.config.{js,cjs,mjs,ts}` exists with no v4 setup (no `tailwindcss` 4 range, no `@import \"tailwindcss\"` stylesheet, no `@tailwindcss/*` package) — exiting 1 and pointing at the official upgrade tool with the project's own runner (`bunx`/`pnpm dlx`/`npx @tailwindcss/upgrade`, https://tailwindcss.com/docs/upgrade-guide). No silent upgrade; `--force` bypasses.",
    "Breaking: every command that reads or writes `components.json` (`add`, `status`, `diff`, `doctor`, `agents sync`, `eject`, `search`, `show`, `registry build`/`list`/`add`/`remove`) now refuses one that belongs to shadcn/ui for React — detected from the owning project (declares `react`, no Marko dependency), not from config fields, because marko-ui itself writes `tsx: true` and the `rsc` key for real Marko projects. Exit 1 says the config is shadcn/ui-for-React and points at `--cwd <app>`, and the file is left byte-identical (previously `registry add` even wrote into it, and a React config parsed as valid so components were installed into a React app). The detector lives in one place — the config loader — and the commands that bypass the loader for partial configs call it directly.",
  ],
};

